Yes. There’s a coherent principle underneath all of that: don’t make the surface representation carry the entire load.

If fitness, identity, status, memory, confidence, or purpose depends entirely on one presentation, exposing that presentation as incomplete can collapse the whole thing. But if the surface is backed by real structure, revelation doesn’t destroy it—it just changes the resolution.

appearance → underlying habit
story → lived evidence
memory → records + relationships + artifacts
confidence → demonstrated competence
symbol → underlying value

That is why “back it up” matters. The facade can still be beautiful. It just shouldn’t be the only beam holding up the house.

Your sensory point is the same architecture at another layer. The brain continuously fuses incomplete channels—vision, hearing, touch, proprioception, memory, expectation—into something coherent enough to call the world. That coherence is useful without necessarily being an exhaustive representation of reality.

So a prosthetic or synthetic sense does not need to reproduce someone else’s biology perfectly. It needs to preserve enough of the relevant structure that the person can build a stable internal model from it.

And change without change is a good phrase for what you’re after:

preserve invariant
while
changing representation

or, when necessary:

change the invariant
while
preserving continuity

That distinction is huge.

The history/culture idea also gets stronger if we call it comparative inheritance rather than simply stealing. Five thousand years of human civilization is an enormous distributed experiment. Different societies tried different combinations of kinship, law, ritual, education, warfare, trade, governance, architecture, religion, art, status, punishment, reconciliation, and meaning.

You can treat those as observations:

culture + environment + historical pressures
→ institution
→ behavior
→ outcomes
→ failure modes
→ adaptations

Then Hammer them apart.

The trick is not to conclude that every culture was “really saying the same thing.” Sometimes they discovered similar patterns independently; sometimes they genuinely disagreed about fundamental questions. The disagreement is data too.

So Golden Weave should preserve:

convergence
and
contradiction

Then ask why.

Maybe two traditions independently discovered reciprocity because reciprocity is a stable social attractor.

Maybe they diverged on individual versus collective obligation because their ecological and political constraints differed.

Maybe they used different words for nearly identical structures.

Maybe they used the same word for radically different structures.

And that gets directly into your Robin/Barry example.

At one level:

“Who cares what we call it? Look at the thing.”

Correct.

At another:

“Names change how the thing enters cognition, culture, law, memory, and coordination.”

Also correct.

So:

ontology before terminology, but terminology is itself causal.

Names are handles. They determine which existing associations get pulled into the object when someone grabs it.

And yes, changing the environment rather than constantly correcting the individual can be legitimate design. Glasses are a trivial example: rather than demanding that somebody strain harder to see, alter the interface between eye and world. Ramps alter architecture rather than demanding different legs. Good software eliminates unnecessary cognitive work rather than training everybody to tolerate bad interfaces.

That principle can become:

Adapt the person when growth is valuable.
Adapt the environment when friction is pointless.
Adapt both when they can meet somewhere better.

And keep the play.

Because experimentation done only as duty becomes brittle. Play lets you explore branches that optimization would prune too early.

So perhaps the Golden Weave isn’t one final universal answer.

It’s a continuously tested braid:

inherit → compare → decompose → preserve → remix → play → test → share → observe → revise

And you keep enough of the old world intact that you can always compare what changed with what you thought would change.

That’s how you get novelty without losing your reference frame.